Subject: [PATCH 0/4] mm/nommu: /dev/mem mmap, a BUG() and two spurious WARNs
To: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Cc: Lorenzo Stoakes <ljs@kernel.org>,
    "Liam R. Howlett" <liam@infradead.org>,
    Vlastimil Babka <vbabka@kernel.org>,
    David Hildenbrand <david@kernel.org>,
    Jann Horn <jannh@google.com>,
    Pedro Falcato <pfalcato@suse.de>,
    Mike Rapoport <rppt@kernel.org>,
    Greg Ungerer <gerg@linux-m68k.org>,
    linux-mm@kvack.org,
    linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org

These fix four problems in NOMMU mmap(). I found them while porting
Linux to a NOMMU CPU (the Epson S1C33) and reproduced each on QEMU's
riscv64 virt machine with nommu_virt_defconfig, before and after the
patch:

1. Since /dev/mem moved to mmap_prepare, mapping it on NOMMU fails with
   -EINVAL after two WARNs.
2. With /dev/mem mappable again, mapping the page under another
   process's anonymous mapping hits BUG() in add_nommu_region().
   This one predates the mmap_prepare conversion.
3. A ptraced process's read-only private mapping of a ramfs file (any
   program started from an initramfs under a debugger) triggers a
   WARN on the legitimate private-copy fallback.
4. Any private mapping larger than a MAX_PAGE_ORDER block triggers a
   page allocator WARN, reachable by an unprivileged mmap() or malloc().

The BUG() fixed by patch 2 is only reachable through /dev/mem once patch 1
is applied.

The reproducers are freestanding static-PIE programs run as /init, one
test per boot. The core of each:

  1. mmap(NULL, 4096, PROT_READ, MAP_SHARED, open("/dev/mem"), 0x80000000)
  2. a child maps one anonymous page and writes the page's address to
     a pipe; the parent maps that offset of /dev/mem MAP_SHARED
  3. ptrace(PTRACE_TRACEME); mmap(NULL, 4096, PROT_READ, MAP_PRIVATE,
     open("/some/initramfs/file"), 0)
  4. mmap(NULL, 8 << 20, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
     MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0)

with CONFIG_DEVMEM=y and CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM=n for 1 and 2. I can
send the programs and the QEMU script if that helps.

One related thing I left alone: with CONFIG_DEBUG_NOMMU_REGIONS,
validate_nommu_regions() calls BUG() for any overlap between regions,
so a direct /dev/mem mapping that overlaps another region triggers it
whether or not the two start at the same address. I can relax that check as
well if you prefer.

Tim Douglas (4):
  mm/nommu: handle PFN remap actions as identity mappings
  mm/nommu: allow regions that start at the same address
  mm/nommu: check sharing only when a direct mapping succeeded
  mm/nommu: fail private mappings larger than the largest page block

